    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d425">

                <pb facs="0628" n="534" sameAs="frus:frus1896/pg534"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Olney to Baron von Thielmann.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1896-04-03"><hi rendition="#italic">April 3,
                        1896</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 131.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the
                    receipt of your note of the 23d ultimo, touching the insufficiency of the Samoan
                    revenues and the dissatisfaction expressed by the foreign settlers with the
                    present system of taxation.</p>
                <p>You accordingly solicit a statement of the intentions of the Government of the
                    United States with reference to the adoption of any measures that may afford
                    relief.</p>
                <p>In reply I have to say that this Government has formed no plan, and therefore has
                    at present no suggestions to offer as a remedy for the state of affairs said to
                    exist in the Samoan Islands in relation to the subject of native taxation.</p>
                <p>Accept, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Richard Olney.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
